    {
      "bbox": [
        141,
        1325,
        1919,
        1982
      ],
      "category": "Text",
      "text": "4. REVIEW OF SCRIPTS: Unless a law is being violated or the public interest is endangered, a municipal official should not refuse to allow motion picture and television production companies the use of public facilities because the official does not approve of a script. The existing laws will be relied upon to safeguard the public interest in this area without resorting to municipal censorship. Moreover, the withholding of the use of a public facility does not preclude the production of the movie or television film. The company can simply move to another city, taking its business with it; film the script, and still depict it as taking place in New York. Henceforth, scripts should not be examined for the purpose of editorial censorship and the use of public facilities should no longer be denied on this ground.",
